1. Download all the VirusShare files. They are cumulative, so if you've kept
the previous ones, you need only download the new ones.

curl -O https://virusshare.com/hashfiles/VirusShare_[00000-00457].md5

2. To make the hashset:

sed -e '/^#/d' VirusShare_* | sort -u >vs_hashes
mkhashset 'VirusShare 0-457' 'VirusShare 0-457' MD5 false true vs_hashes virusshare-457.hset

3. Upload the hashset to the E2E testdata bucket in S3.


Hashing the VirusShare sample archives (vsscan)
===============================================

The VirusShare_NNNNN.zip archives hold the samples themselves, encrypted
with the password "infected" (ZipCrypto, deflate). src/vsscan/vsscan (built
when configure finds libarchive) hashes every member in memory -- nothing
from an archive is written to disk or executed -- and builds an hset from
the results.

1. Scan. One zip at a time, 8 libarchive readers per zip, one hashing
thread per CPU; outputs go to OUTDIR:

  vsscan scan -o OUTDIR /data/VirusShare/VirusShare_000*.zip

  OUTDIR/VirusShare_NNNNN.tsv          one row per sample (header line first):
      zip index member size md5 sha1 sha256 tlsh ssdeep entropy
      tlsh is "T1" + 70 hex digits or TNULL (under 50 bytes, too little
      variety); member names are backslash-escaped (\t \n \r \\)
  OUTDIR/VirusShare_NNNNN.errors.tsv   per-entry errors and warnings:
      zip index member stage message
      stages: open, header, data (bad password, bad CRC, unsupported
      method), data-fatal (corrupt stream; the zip is reopened and the scan
      resumes after the entry), name-md5-mismatch (a warning: the member is
      named VirusShare_<md5> and its md5 differs), not-regular
  OUTDIR/VirusShare_NNNNN.summary.tsv  written last, when the zip is done:
      zip status entries rows errors name_md5_mismatches bytes zip_bytes
      seconds finished;  status is complete, partial (--limit or
      --time-limit) or failed

Each zip's TSV and errors file are written as *.partial and renamed when the
zip is finished. Rerunning the same command skips every zip whose summary
says "complete" (--force rescans), so an interrupted scan resumes at the
first unfinished zip. Progress goes to stderr every 10 s (-P): entries/s,
MB/s through the zip files and of hashed (uncompressed) data, CPU, bytes
buffered for hashing, errors. vsscan exits 1 if any zip failed.

Run it under nohup or tmux, e.g.

  nohup vsscan scan -o /work/vs-eval/scan /data/VirusShare/VirusShare_000*.zip \
    > /work/vs-eval/scan.log 2>&1 &

Useful options: -n N / -T SECS stop each zip after N entries / the scan
after SECS seconds (a quick check that an archive decrypts); -r N readers
per zip; -j N zips at once; -t N hashing threads; -m MiB of sample data
buffered between the readers and the hashing threads (default 2048; a
sample larger than that is read and hashed alone); --streaming reads a zip
without its central directory (a truncated copy), with one reader and no
recovery after a corrupt entry.

Why these defaults. On the virgil VM (16 vCPU Westmere E5645, no SHA-NI or
AVX; RAID5 over virtiofs) the scan is CPU-bound at about 130-140 MB/s of
sample data (90-115 MB/s through the zips). A third of the CPU goes to the
readers: libarchive decrypts ZipCrypto one byte at a time through zlib's
crc32, which costs more than the inflate, and does both on the thread that
reads the entry. So each zip has several readers (-r), each with its own
libarchive handle taking every r-th entry and seeking over the others; they
move through the file together and a prefetch thread reads 512 MiB ahead
of them with large reads, so the disk sees one sequential stream. The rest
goes to hashing (TLSH and ssdeep about a quarter each, SHA-256 8%). Two
zips at once (-j 2) were slower than one (about 70 MB/s against 95-113
MB/s through the zips): the RAID streams one file at 300-450 MB/s but
seeks between two.

vsscan sets TZ=UTC when TZ is unset: libarchive calls mktime for every
entry's timestamp, and with no TZ and no /etc/localtime (as on the VM)
glibc tries to open /etc/localtime on every call under its global lock,
which serialized the readers (131,072 failed opens per zip and reader).

The 72 zips hold 9,437,183 samples, 3.82 TB uncompressed in 2.88 TB of
zips; the first, VirusShare_00000.zip (131,072 samples, 21.6 GB in a 14.6
GB zip), scans in 165 s with no errors.

2. Build the hset. The hset record format stores fixed-length hash types
only, so a record holds md5, sha1, sha256, tlsh and size (TNULL: field
absent, no TLSH row); the file gets the md5, sha1, sha2_256, tlsh and sizes
hash sections, and the TLSH section its TVEC embedding vectors and TIVF
kNN index. ssdeep, entropy, zip and member name are not representable in
the hset and stay in the TSVs, which are the full-fidelity sidecar (join on
sha256 or md5).

  vsscan build --name 'VirusShare 0-71' --desc 'VirusShare_00000-00071 samples' \
    OUTDIR/virusshare-0-71.hset OUTDIR

An INPUT directory means its data TSVs (not *.errors.tsv or *.summary.tsv);
TSV files can also be listed. Identical records collapse to one.
HSET_TLSH_OPTIONS or --tlsh-options select the TLSH sections as for
mkhashset (default 3, both).

Build cost, measured at full scale on the VM (9,437,184 synthetic rows: the
131,072 real TLSH digests of VirusShare_00000 in 72 copies with a few body
nibbles mutated per copy, random md5/sha1/sha256): 411 s in all, 103 s of it
parsing the TSVs; then the builder sorts the records, builds the TIVF index
on all CPUs and writes the hash sections, RIDX, TVEC, TIVF and the file
SHA-256. The file is 12.5 GB (TVEC is 9.9 GB of it). The builder works in a
shared mapping of the output file: its anonymous memory peaked at 420 MB,
and the 12 GB of file pages it touched (maxrss 12.4 GB) are page cache that
the kernel writes back and can reclaim (MemAvailable stayed above 30 GB),
so the whole set builds in one pass in 31 GB of RAM, with no per-zip hsets
or merges. The output's file system needs room for the file and nothing
else (no temp files with records).

The same records can be built with mkhashset from the TSVs (size must be
the last column: mkhashset parses a size to the end of the line):

  for f in OUTDIR/VirusShare_000*[0-9].tsv ; do tail -n +2 $f ; done |
    awk -F'\t' '{print $5, $6, $7, $8, $4}' >vs.txt
  mkhashset 'VirusShare 0-71' 'VirusShare_00000-00071 samples' \
    md5 sha1 sha2256 tlsh size true true vs.txt virusshare-0-71.hset
